From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:59:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A torch's glow is hidden by its center, not cut by its plane

Patch 0050 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user, 2026-10-08, in the Fire Temple: "the radial glows that are around ... things like torches
or navi ... rotate. They're just a flat surface that rotates around to basically always face the
camera. So they're not an actual 3D sphere ... at certain angles, that plane starts to get clipped
by the geometry around it", and from the Inspect view of a torch in a recess: "you can see its that
the outer glow is clipping behing the walls and ceiling geometry".

Every point light's glow is Lights_DrawGlow's quad, turned to the eye and about 230 units across at
a torch's radius, drawn with no depth test (G_RM_CLD_SURF) after the rooms. 0028 and 0040 take it as
an overlay and 0046, made for Navi's core, depth tests every overlay against the world, so a flame
in a recess showed its glow only through the opening, edged by the lintel and the jamb: a
regression of 0.4.0. The console tested only whether the light's center was covered.
patches/light_glow.c now draws each glow inside RT64's vertex Z test at the light's center (the
reference project's technique), and a draw inside that test takes no depth test here.

The Debugging menu's Glow test row chooses, in tuneLightTest.y, read on the CPU (the game patch
reads the same row): 0 glows by their center, Navi's core still tested per pixel; 1 glows by their
center, nothing tested per pixel; 2 every overlay tested per pixel and no center test, as 0.4.0;
3 nothing tested at all, as 0.3.9.

diff --git a/src/shared/rt64_raytracing_params.h b/src/shared/rt64_raytracing_params.h
--- a/src/shared/rt64_raytracing_params.h
+++ b/src/shared/rt64_raytracing_params.h
@@ -113,7 +113,7 @@ namespace interop {
         float4 tuneWindowAim;    // patch 0044, read on the CPU: x degrees steeper, y degrees turned, z the window aimed (0 every one), w 1 to start from the drawn shaft's direction
         float4 tuneWindowMove;   // patch 0044, read on the CPU: x, y, z world units the aimed window's light is moved
         float4 tuneWindField;    // patch 0047, set on the CPU every trace: xyz how far the wind has carried the dust so far (world units), w the dust's clock in displayed seconds (patch 0048)
-        float4 tuneLightTest;    // patch 0049, two Debugging rows of candidates: x the Light through stone row (0 stops at the first surface, glow in the air too; 1 the same, glow unshadowed; 2 the 48 unit clearance, as 0.4.0)
+        float4 tuneLightTest;    // patch 0049, two Debugging rows of candidates: x the Light through stone row (0 stops at the first surface, glow in the air too; 1 the same, glow unshadowed; 2 the 48 unit clearance, as 0.4.0); patch 0050, read on the CPU: y the Glow test row (see overlayDepthTest)
 
 #   ifdef HLSL_CPU
         RaytracingParams() {
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.h b/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.h
--- a/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.h
+++ b/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.h
@@ -130,7 +130,10 @@ namespace RT64 {
         // at the first surface it meets and so does its glow in the air, 1 the same but the glow
         // unshadowed, 2 the 48 unit clearance as it was in 0.4.0.
         int lightStone = 0;
-        int shadowRays = 4;                 // the Shadow rays row (2026-09-24): samples over the sun's disc per lit pixel, a moving surface twice
+        // The Glow test row from the Debugging menu (patch 0050): how a glow laid over the picture
+        // is hidden by what stands in front of it (rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp, overlayDepthTest).
+        int glowTest = 0;
+        int shadowRays = 4;                // the Shadow rays row (2026-09-24): samples over the sun's disc per lit pixel, a moving surface twice
         float indoorLight = 0.5f;            // the Indoor light row (2026-09-24): a sun of our own where the place has no sky, as a share of the room's ambient taken out of it
         int motionBlurSamples = 0;
         float motionBlurStrength = 0.0f;
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.cpp b/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_raytracing_resources.cpp
@@ -854,7 +854,7 @@ namespace RT64 {
         rtParams.tuneWindowMove = hlslpp::float4(std::clamp(rtConfig.windowMoveX, -1000.0f, 1000.0f), std::clamp(rtConfig.windowMoveY, -1000.0f, 1000.0f),
                                                  std::clamp(rtConfig.windowMoveZ, -1000.0f, 1000.0f), 0.0f);
         // The candidates the Debugging menu cycles (patch 0049).
-        rtParams.tuneLightTest = hlslpp::float4(float(std::clamp(rtConfig.lightStone, 0, 2)), 0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
+        rtParams.tuneLightTest = hlslpp::float4(float(std::clamp(rtConfig.lightStone, 0, 2)), float(std::clamp(rtConfig.glowTest, 0, 3)), 0.0f, 0.0f);
         rtParams.tuneScatter = hlslpp::float4(rtConfig.lightGlow, rtConfig.sunHaze, rtConfig.hazeRange, rtConfig.lightReach);
         rtParams.tuneFade = hlslpp::float4(rtConfig.fogFade, rtConfig.distanceFade, rtConfig.shadowSoftness, rtConfig.historyBlend);
         // y the Darkness row, z the Shadow rays row: how many samples over the sun's disc every
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -2810,7 +2810,32 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         // is now drawn with a depth test against the world drawn before it (compare,
                         // never write): still untouched by the lighting, but hidden where Link, a
                         // character or a wall stands in front of it.
-                        overlayDepthTest = depthlessOverlay;
+                        //
+                        // A TORCH'S GLOW IS NOT A FLAT CARD IN THE WALL (patch 0050, the user, 2026-10-08,
+                        // in the Fire Temple: "the radial glows that are around ... things like torches or
+                        // navi ... rotate. They're just a flat surface that rotates around to basically
+                        // always face the camera ... so at certain angles, that plane starts to get
+                        // clipped by the geometry around it"; "you can see its that the outer glow is
+                        // clipping behing the walls and ceiling geometry"). Every point light's glow
+                        // (Lights_DrawGlow: one quad turned to the eye, about 230 units across at a
+                        // torch's radius) is an overlay too, and the test above cut it wherever stone
+                        // stood in front of its plane: a recessed flame's glow showed only through the
+                        // recess opening, edged by the lintel and the jamb. The console never tested
+                        // it per pixel; the game asked once whether the light's center was covered.
+                        // patches/light_glow.c now asks the renderer the same question (the vertex Z
+                        // test, the reference project's way, at the real frame's width), so a draw
+                        // inside that test is hidden whole or drawn whole and takes no test here.
+                        //
+                        // As candidates on the Debugging menu's Glow test row (the user: "adds some
+                        // debugging options to cycle through ... which one fixes it without
+                        // regressions"), tuneLightTest.y, the patch reading the same row:
+                        //   0  glows by their center; Navi's core still tested here, as since 0046
+                        //   1  glows by their center; nothing tested here, Navi's core included
+                        //   2  every overlay tested here and no center test: 0.4.0
+                        //   3  nothing tested anywhere: 0.3.9
+                        const int glowTest = int(rtResources->rtParams.tuneLightTest.y);
+                        const bool insideCenterTest = (vertexTestZCallIndex >= 0);
+                        overlayDepthTest = depthlessOverlay && ((glowTest == 2) || ((glowTest == 0) && !insideCenterTest));
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
                         const bool wasTranslucent = (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU);
                         if ((wasTranslucent && !solidDraw) || depthlessOverlay) {
